WEBVTT

00:00:04.430 --> 00:00:09.550
Welcome to ABE Testing Podcast, your modern testing podcast.

00:00:09.550 --> 00:00:15.630
Your hosts, Alan and Brent, will be here to guide you through topics on testing, leadership,

00:00:15.630 --> 00:00:18.310
agile, and anything else that comes to mind.

00:00:18.310 --> 00:00:20.900
Now on with the show.

00:00:20.900 --> 00:00:22.740
Hey everyone, it's Alan.

00:00:22.740 --> 00:00:24.060
Brent is not here.

00:00:24.060 --> 00:00:30.620
Brent is actually working on a spaceship that Satya is building powered entirely

00:00:30.620 --> 00:00:31.620
by GPUs.

00:00:31.620 --> 00:00:33.780
It's going to be interesting to see how that comes out.

00:00:33.900 --> 00:00:35.580
He'll be back soon.

00:00:35.580 --> 00:00:37.940
We have a lot of stuff to keep talking about.

00:00:37.940 --> 00:00:41.260
I was thinking just during the intro, it's the modern testing podcast.

00:00:41.260 --> 00:00:42.460
We haven't talked.

00:00:42.460 --> 00:00:45.380
Again, modern testing principles aren't that modern, aren't about testing.

00:00:45.380 --> 00:00:46.540
Sometimes we talk about testing.

00:00:46.540 --> 00:00:49.380
A lot of times we just talk about life, leadership, everything else.

00:00:49.380 --> 00:00:52.580
We're going to do a little bit of all of that today because I have a guest.

00:00:52.580 --> 00:00:55.020
Aditya Chala is here with me today.

00:00:55.020 --> 00:00:59.540
So Aditya, introduce yourself and then we'll get going on a great conversation today.

00:00:59.540 --> 00:01:01.220
Say hello and introduce yourself.

00:01:01.220 --> 00:01:02.460
Yeah, hello everybody.

00:01:02.460 --> 00:01:03.580
Thank you, Alan.

00:01:03.660 --> 00:01:05.660
And thank you for having me here.

00:01:05.660 --> 00:01:08.580
I'm very excited about the conversation you're going to have.

00:01:08.580 --> 00:01:09.780
I'm Aditya Chala.

00:01:09.780 --> 00:01:11.820
I'm co-founder of QA pilot.

00:01:11.820 --> 00:01:15.740
We are a mobile app testing platform.

00:01:15.740 --> 00:01:20.620
Our genesis is that we think that most testing platforms focus on web,

00:01:20.620 --> 00:01:22.180
not enough on mobile.

00:01:22.180 --> 00:01:26.180
And that's essentially what got us to building this platform

00:01:26.180 --> 00:01:29.980
because my co-founder has experience in delivering, you know,

00:01:29.980 --> 00:01:33.020
large enterprise mobile app which are used by hundreds of millions of people

00:01:33.020 --> 00:01:38.220
and he found the gap in the market that there's no real good

00:01:38.220 --> 00:01:39.980
mobile app testing platform.

00:01:39.980 --> 00:01:42.420
And that's really why we started building QA pilot.

00:01:42.420 --> 00:01:46.860
And that's also, I think we're seeing a reasonable amount of traction

00:01:46.860 --> 00:01:51.440
because it's genuinely an underserved part of the market.

00:01:51.440 --> 00:01:52.960
Yeah, yeah, that's interesting.

00:01:52.960 --> 00:01:55.160
So we'll talk a little bit about QA pilot.

00:01:55.160 --> 00:01:56.680
I'm not going to stop you from talking about it.

00:01:56.680 --> 00:02:01.160
But I really want to talk about a lot today is challenges in mobile testing

00:02:01.160 --> 00:02:03.240
because the challenges continue.

00:02:03.280 --> 00:02:04.120
It's always been hard.

00:02:04.120 --> 00:02:05.840
Challenges continue to grow.

00:02:05.840 --> 00:02:10.320
And then there's this I'm not sure if our listeners have heard of this thing

00:02:10.320 --> 00:02:15.400
called AI that is changing the way software is being developed massively.

00:02:15.400 --> 00:02:17.960
So we're dealing with that, too.

00:02:17.960 --> 00:02:22.920
So why is so let's just kind of get into it.

00:02:22.920 --> 00:02:24.680
Let's talk about mobile testing.

00:02:24.680 --> 00:02:27.160
So I got my phone here.

00:02:27.160 --> 00:02:28.640
You may have a different phone.

00:02:28.640 --> 00:02:32.160
In fact, everyone has a different phone because there are ten thousand

00:02:32.160 --> 00:02:35.080
different phones out in there, and most of them are Android or Apple,

00:02:35.080 --> 00:02:37.280
but not all of them are in the different versions of Android.

00:02:37.280 --> 00:02:38.080
They use different things.

00:02:38.080 --> 00:02:41.640
There's different apps and there's a gazillion apps each of the app stores.

00:02:41.960 --> 00:02:44.800
So other than that,

00:02:44.800 --> 00:02:46.960
why is mobile testing so difficult these days?

00:02:46.960 --> 00:02:49.200
And why? Actually, I shouldn't even say these days.

00:02:49.200 --> 00:02:52.440
In general, why is mobile testing difficult?

00:02:52.440 --> 00:02:54.420
And maybe not talked about enough.

00:02:54.420 --> 00:02:58.700
Yeah, let me try and I mean, we work on this together, right?

00:02:58.700 --> 00:03:02.180
I mean, I'm sure you have. Yeah, I have my ideas.

00:03:02.180 --> 00:03:06.780
Yeah, so we'll start from the beginning, right?

00:03:06.780 --> 00:03:07.980
It's almost like a history lesson.

00:03:07.980 --> 00:03:14.380
So most web testing tools or UI testing tools start with having a good idea

00:03:14.380 --> 00:03:15.980
of what locators are, right?

00:03:15.980 --> 00:03:20.220
You need to be able to locate an element on a screen or some user interface.

00:03:20.740 --> 00:03:22.740
And the next time you come onto it for testing,

00:03:22.740 --> 00:03:26.060
you should be able to identify it with a reasonable degree of confidence.

00:03:26.460 --> 00:03:29.760
Right. And all testing tools

00:03:30.160 --> 00:03:32.560
essentially expect to be able to do that.

00:03:33.160 --> 00:03:36.800
And for that, they use something called the DOM in web.

00:03:36.800 --> 00:03:40.080
And there are a whole bunch of other things which have been built around it.

00:03:40.280 --> 00:03:42.400
There are accessibility trees and so on.

00:03:42.400 --> 00:03:45.760
And if the developers use the accessibility trees on the DOM effectively,

00:03:46.240 --> 00:03:49.640
then you can write your test scripts in a less flaky,

00:03:49.640 --> 00:03:52.440
less brittle manner, and they stand the test of time.

00:03:53.120 --> 00:03:57.120
Now, that paradigm, when you move to mobile,

00:03:58.840 --> 00:04:01.440
doesn't work very well for a variety of reasons, right?

00:04:01.440 --> 00:04:06.920
Because one, most browsers are broadly the same.

00:04:07.440 --> 00:04:09.600
Most websites are broadly the same.

00:04:10.760 --> 00:04:14.600
And they, you can also test them on headless browsers.

00:04:15.040 --> 00:04:18.000
Whereas in mobile, you have two gates, right?

00:04:18.000 --> 00:04:20.960
You have the Apple gate, the Android gate, and within Android,

00:04:20.960 --> 00:04:22.560
you have different flavors.

00:04:22.560 --> 00:04:24.840
And you can build your apps in different ways

00:04:24.840 --> 00:04:26.880
and not all of them follow the same structure.

00:04:27.240 --> 00:04:29.480
So creating this automation becomes a problem

00:04:30.080 --> 00:04:34.970
in a way in which it is reliable,

00:04:35.800 --> 00:04:38.480
not flaky, not brittle, right? That's one.

00:04:38.640 --> 00:04:42.660
The second issue you have is with mobile.

00:04:43.340 --> 00:04:47.060
You also have, like you said, the proliferation of OS versions,

00:04:47.060 --> 00:04:49.460
Android, iOS devices.

00:04:49.460 --> 00:04:51.460
You have to test on real devices.

00:04:51.580 --> 00:04:53.580
And that adds another level of complexity.

00:04:54.820 --> 00:04:57.300
And then finally, I think this will be interesting,

00:04:57.300 --> 00:05:00.700
which we should probably speak about more today,

00:05:00.700 --> 00:05:05.120
is what AI is doing and what cloud

00:05:05.120 --> 00:05:09.620
and all of the other tools did is collapse the SDLC,

00:05:09.620 --> 00:05:11.380
the software development lifecycle.

00:05:11.380 --> 00:05:12.820
AI is doing that even more, right?

00:05:12.820 --> 00:05:14.820
You won't be able to deploy multiple times.

00:05:14.820 --> 00:05:16.260
But you can't do that in mobile.

00:05:16.260 --> 00:05:18.020
Mobile, you put a mobile app,

00:05:18.020 --> 00:05:20.900
somebody has to approve it for it to go onto the app store.

00:05:20.900 --> 00:05:23.260
And then the consumer has to download it.

00:05:23.260 --> 00:05:26.100
So unlike web, where you push it into production

00:05:26.100 --> 00:05:29.060
and the consumer comes on and gets the latest version,

00:05:29.060 --> 00:05:30.540
in mobile, there's a lag.

00:05:30.540 --> 00:05:34.060
And I think this is an underappreciated part of the SDLC

00:05:34.060 --> 00:05:35.780
and what's happening with AI and SDLC,

00:05:35.780 --> 00:05:39.900
that all the tools which talk about all the changes in SDLC

00:05:39.900 --> 00:05:42.300
just don't talk about this aspect of mobile.

00:05:42.300 --> 00:05:45.420
And I think all of this just makes mobile a different beast.

00:05:46.060 --> 00:05:47.740
And that was more complex,

00:05:48.500 --> 00:05:52.180
that you can't take what you've learned in other types of testing

00:05:52.180 --> 00:05:53.780
and apply it blindly on mobile.

00:05:54.960 --> 00:05:57.160
I think you can't.

00:05:57.160 --> 00:05:58.720
And that applies to desktops as well.

00:05:58.720 --> 00:06:01.000
I'm thinking while I talk, which happens sometimes,

00:06:01.000 --> 00:06:03.400
and it may or may not make sense.

00:06:03.400 --> 00:06:07.430
A lot of, you're right about apps,

00:06:07.430 --> 00:06:11.110
you can't update, like you can't push an update

00:06:11.110 --> 00:06:14.190
to a user every five minutes like you can with a web page.

00:06:14.190 --> 00:06:15.270
Yeah.

00:06:15.310 --> 00:06:20.380
I think some applications use a lot of content

00:06:20.380 --> 00:06:25.260
from the internet, or they download in real time,

00:06:25.260 --> 00:06:26.300
so you can make those changes.

00:06:26.300 --> 00:06:27.620
But at the same time,

00:06:28.620 --> 00:06:30.500
phones don't always have internet connections

00:06:30.500 --> 00:06:32.140
and no one wants to ship an app that won't work

00:06:32.140 --> 00:06:33.340
if you don't have internet connection.

00:06:33.340 --> 00:06:36.580
So it is interesting there.

00:06:36.580 --> 00:06:39.740
So as fast as AI development can go,

00:06:40.300 --> 00:06:43.940
you are limited in how often you can update your users.

00:06:43.940 --> 00:06:45.580
And I'm thinking, this is true,

00:06:45.580 --> 00:06:47.700
I'm just thinking this while I'm saying it.

00:06:47.700 --> 00:06:51.940
So as much as now with AI speed development,

00:06:51.940 --> 00:06:54.500
it's got to be a better word for that.

00:06:54.500 --> 00:06:55.900
You can develop features much faster

00:06:55.900 --> 00:06:58.420
as a matter of web pages, applications, whatever.

00:06:59.460 --> 00:07:06.460
If I'm only able to push an update to my users once a week,

00:07:06.460 --> 00:07:09.180
maybe more often if they're in pilot programs,

00:07:09.180 --> 00:07:10.740
maybe less often if they're,

00:07:10.740 --> 00:07:13.760
because it's their choice when to update,

00:07:13.800 --> 00:07:15.480
I can get them a brand,

00:07:15.480 --> 00:07:18.080
it could be a whole new application in two weeks.

00:07:18.600 --> 00:07:19.640
Exactly.

00:07:19.640 --> 00:07:26.140
And so it's not only a functional testing issue,

00:07:26.140 --> 00:07:28.620
it's like you can't do it.

00:07:28.620 --> 00:07:29.940
I'm thinking about the user perspective,

00:07:29.940 --> 00:07:32.940
you can't do it because if I go to open,

00:07:34.820 --> 00:07:37.300
I freaked out when they changed my Spotify icon.

00:07:37.300 --> 00:07:39.100
The app was the same.

00:07:39.100 --> 00:07:41.300
Like, what do you do?

00:07:41.300 --> 00:07:42.140
I want to talk about,

00:07:42.140 --> 00:07:43.460
I'm going to get back to the testing side of this,

00:07:43.500 --> 00:07:46.380
but I haven't thought about this deeply until this moment

00:07:46.380 --> 00:07:51.680
is all the AI tools give you the ability to move so fast

00:07:51.680 --> 00:07:53.320
and change so much so quickly.

00:07:54.580 --> 00:07:56.220
Regardless of the testing problem,

00:07:56.220 --> 00:07:59.180
how do you resist not adding nine features

00:07:59.180 --> 00:08:01.260
to your mobile application over the pace of a week?

00:08:01.260 --> 00:08:03.300
And you may not ask your opinion on that,

00:08:03.300 --> 00:08:05.500
but like, what do you do?

00:08:05.500 --> 00:08:07.860
Like I could, if I was an app developer,

00:08:07.860 --> 00:08:09.820
I could just keep on adding features and things

00:08:09.820 --> 00:08:13.100
and just kind of go wild in a way

00:08:13.100 --> 00:08:14.540
that I could never have done before

00:08:15.380 --> 00:08:17.740
and yet it's a lot of shock for a user

00:08:17.740 --> 00:08:19.260
as they download the new application

00:08:19.260 --> 00:08:20.300
and it has eight new features

00:08:20.300 --> 00:08:21.730
and they don't know how to use them.

00:08:21.730 --> 00:08:22.930
No, absolutely.

00:08:22.930 --> 00:08:24.770
Let me add more complexity in this, right?

00:08:24.770 --> 00:08:28.250
And I think you touched on it,

00:08:28.250 --> 00:08:31.260
which is there's web functionality,

00:08:31.260 --> 00:08:32.340
which is in mobile apps, right?

00:08:32.340 --> 00:08:34.380
You have this content which is coming in.

00:08:34.380 --> 00:08:39.060
Now you have parts of your SDLC or your CI, CD pipelines

00:08:39.060 --> 00:08:42.420
which push into production every five minutes

00:08:42.420 --> 00:08:44.580
or can push in every day

00:08:45.500 --> 00:08:47.220
and mobile is a different pace, right?

00:08:47.220 --> 00:08:50.540
Both are moving at different velocities across your team.

00:08:50.540 --> 00:08:52.900
So it also becomes a management problem

00:08:52.900 --> 00:08:56.890
where the risk which you see in web

00:08:56.890 --> 00:08:59.570
is very different from how you perceive risk on mobile.

00:08:59.570 --> 00:09:02.770
And therefore, because,

00:09:02.770 --> 00:09:04.330
and I don't think we speak about that enough, right?

00:09:04.330 --> 00:09:06.570
Because in web, you can fix forward,

00:09:06.570 --> 00:09:10.050
you can launch, you find a mistake, you fix it.

00:09:10.050 --> 00:09:11.930
And therefore, the amount of testing you do,

00:09:11.970 --> 00:09:14.890
the tools you need, the way the SRE team

00:09:14.890 --> 00:09:17.050
and the dev team and the mobile team,

00:09:17.050 --> 00:09:19.330
the QA team work together is very different

00:09:19.330 --> 00:09:23.250
from how mobile should work for the same amount of risk.

00:09:23.250 --> 00:09:28.120
And it's something which is, I think, changing

00:09:28.120 --> 00:09:30.520
and something which I hope

00:09:30.520 --> 00:09:32.520
that we'll have a better answer in a year

00:09:32.520 --> 00:09:35.360
and that because of what my focus,

00:09:37.390 --> 00:09:41.430
I'll be in the midst of this change, right?

00:09:41.430 --> 00:09:43.110
So how do we test it?

00:09:44.100 --> 00:09:46.220
Even so, let's say,

00:09:47.060 --> 00:09:49.740
let's be more realistic for the user.

00:09:49.740 --> 00:09:51.300
We're still, in the space of a week,

00:09:51.300 --> 00:09:55.180
we can still add or fix or change or enhance

00:09:55.180 --> 00:09:56.860
major functionality in a week.

00:09:59.220 --> 00:10:01.980
In my experience, the folks,

00:10:01.980 --> 00:10:04.340
well, I'll try to give a leading answer here.

00:10:05.740 --> 00:10:09.140
How does that change how we need to test these things?

00:10:09.140 --> 00:10:10.860
Can we still do script-based automation?

00:10:10.860 --> 00:10:13.300
Can we still, are we using AI?

00:10:14.100 --> 00:10:15.260
How do we keep up?

00:10:15.260 --> 00:10:17.860
How do testers keep up if,

00:10:17.860 --> 00:10:19.100
the thing they're testing this week

00:10:19.100 --> 00:10:21.500
is so much different than the thing they tested last week

00:10:21.500 --> 00:10:25.210
and the release train's gotta keep on going?

00:10:25.210 --> 00:10:27.050
No, I think script-based automation

00:10:27.050 --> 00:10:30.210
still is the bedrock of all automation, right?

00:10:30.210 --> 00:10:31.570
Especially functional UI.

00:10:31.570 --> 00:10:33.090
The other types of automation, but functional,

00:10:33.090 --> 00:10:36.610
I think it's not going anywhere, I would think.

00:10:36.610 --> 00:10:38.450
I mean, as much as people would like to believe

00:10:38.450 --> 00:10:41.250
that visual and it's going to take over, et cetera,

00:10:41.250 --> 00:10:43.450
I think we prefer a hybrid kind of approach

00:10:43.450 --> 00:10:48.830
where there is a script-based where,

00:10:48.830 --> 00:10:52.620
which gives that kind of reliability and consistency

00:10:52.620 --> 00:10:55.620
in testing across multiple devices.

00:10:55.620 --> 00:10:58.430
And then the fallbacks are all hybrid.

00:10:58.430 --> 00:11:00.430
Now, I think what, the other thing which changes,

00:11:00.430 --> 00:11:03.390
and I think which testers have always complained about,

00:11:03.390 --> 00:11:05.550
which make, which become particularly easy

00:11:05.550 --> 00:11:07.750
in today's AI world and AI ID world,

00:11:07.750 --> 00:11:09.030
is that testers complain

00:11:09.030 --> 00:11:12.450
when elements don't have proper IDs, right?

00:11:12.450 --> 00:11:14.290
And that's something that you hear testers do all day.

00:11:14.290 --> 00:11:16.650
Yeah, that's been true in web automation forever, yeah.

00:11:16.650 --> 00:11:20.850
Forever, but in today's world of AI ID,

00:11:20.850 --> 00:11:24.530
it's just one prompt to get the correct locators

00:11:24.530 --> 00:11:29.530
added to your web or your mobile application, right?

00:11:30.210 --> 00:11:32.530
So there are things which make life easier

00:11:32.530 --> 00:11:35.890
because it doesn't require the rigor

00:11:37.700 --> 00:11:39.620
for a developer to actually make life easy

00:11:39.620 --> 00:11:41.420
for a tester who comes downstream.

00:11:41.420 --> 00:11:43.020
It's just a couple of prompts,

00:11:43.020 --> 00:11:46.620
and you'll actually get good IDs across your application,

00:11:46.620 --> 00:11:49.380
which then makes scripting that much more effective.

00:11:49.380 --> 00:11:51.420
So I think scripting is there.

00:11:51.420 --> 00:11:55.260
What we think, we think is that

00:11:55.260 --> 00:11:57.460
there is definitely a place for AI.

00:11:57.460 --> 00:11:59.660
There is definitely a place for

00:11:59.660 --> 00:12:02.060
what can be done without human intervention.

00:12:03.060 --> 00:12:06.980
But the way we see it is that the bulk of the work

00:12:06.980 --> 00:12:09.700
has to be a combination of AI and human,

00:12:09.700 --> 00:12:10.940
so human in the loop.

00:12:11.380 --> 00:12:13.700
And that then doesn't become,

00:12:13.700 --> 00:12:16.740
it's not so much of an AI problem as it's a design problem.

00:12:16.740 --> 00:12:18.940
Because when you're designing the product,

00:12:18.940 --> 00:12:21.540
you need to make sure you bring the human

00:12:21.540 --> 00:12:22.380
in at the right point.

00:12:22.380 --> 00:12:24.980
You give the human the right audit trails.

00:12:24.980 --> 00:12:27.580
You give the human the right visibility

00:12:27.580 --> 00:12:29.980
into what the AI has done to correct AI,

00:12:29.980 --> 00:12:31.500
to verify AI, right?

00:12:31.500 --> 00:12:34.980
And to be able to make the verification cost and time

00:12:34.980 --> 00:12:37.220
lower than what it takes a human

00:12:37.220 --> 00:12:38.660
to actually do it manually.

00:12:38.660 --> 00:12:39.900
Now if you see a lot of tools,

00:12:39.900 --> 00:12:41.220
the complaint is that,

00:12:41.220 --> 00:12:43.060
oh, I could have just done it manually

00:12:43.060 --> 00:12:44.300
in this amount of time.

00:12:44.300 --> 00:12:47.420
So I think it's more of a design problem.

00:12:47.420 --> 00:12:50.460
And just putting a chat bot on products

00:12:50.460 --> 00:12:51.780
is not the way out.

00:12:51.780 --> 00:12:53.140
And I think that's been,

00:12:53.140 --> 00:12:58.430
in my view, where the industry has gone,

00:12:58.430 --> 00:13:01.590
just slap a chat bot onto different parts of the product.

00:13:01.590 --> 00:13:05.030
Well, it's an easy way to tone up modern.

00:13:05.030 --> 00:13:08.110
And then get whatever chat bot

00:13:08.110 --> 00:13:09.830
to write your Python code for you.

00:13:10.830 --> 00:13:13.150
So I'm going to play out something,

00:13:13.150 --> 00:13:14.390
a conversation I've had before,

00:13:14.390 --> 00:13:16.990
because I think it applies here as well.

00:13:16.990 --> 00:13:18.470
And I'm curious on your thoughts.

00:13:18.470 --> 00:13:20.950
I do believe, like here's the challenge.

00:13:20.950 --> 00:13:21.910
I'm not sure what I believe.

00:13:21.910 --> 00:13:23.470
I think I have a belief.

00:13:23.470 --> 00:13:25.630
The challenge is that it doesn't matter

00:13:25.630 --> 00:13:27.550
whether it's mobile where you can ship once a week

00:13:27.550 --> 00:13:29.950
or web where you can ship 50 times a day.

00:13:29.950 --> 00:13:34.140
The pace of development is the same in both places.

00:13:34.140 --> 00:13:37.300
So I talked to a couple of different companies

00:13:37.300 --> 00:13:38.140
about what are they doing

00:13:38.140 --> 00:13:39.140
about the pace of development?

00:13:39.140 --> 00:13:40.620
I talked about, well, do we have,

00:13:40.620 --> 00:13:43.000
it started from, well, we'll have humans

00:13:43.000 --> 00:13:44.760
do all the code reviews.

00:13:44.760 --> 00:13:47.640
And then the humans can't keep up, there's no way.

00:13:47.640 --> 00:13:48.960
Okay, how do we figure out

00:13:48.960 --> 00:13:52.260
which reviews the humans get involved in?

00:13:52.260 --> 00:13:53.100
And they can figure out,

00:13:53.100 --> 00:13:55.540
and even AI can help you find the algorithm for that.

00:13:55.540 --> 00:13:58.380
Or do you pull the humans out completely?

00:13:58.380 --> 00:13:59.220
No, that's too much.

00:13:59.220 --> 00:14:01.180
Maybe it is, maybe it isn't, I don't know.

00:14:01.180 --> 00:14:02.820
And so the challenge is,

00:14:02.820 --> 00:14:05.140
I believe you that we're still in a place

00:14:05.140 --> 00:14:07.300
where we need the human,

00:14:07.300 --> 00:14:11.050
but also inevitably,

00:14:11.050 --> 00:14:15.020
if you use that human even moderately,

00:14:15.020 --> 00:14:17.020
it is the bottle, the human, it,

00:14:17.020 --> 00:14:20.980
the human is the bottleneck in that release process.

00:14:20.980 --> 00:14:22.780
So I'm curious,

00:14:22.780 --> 00:14:24.860
maybe answer two questions at once here,

00:14:24.860 --> 00:14:27.380
but how do you figure out,

00:14:27.380 --> 00:14:29.940
how do you optimize the human involvement

00:14:29.940 --> 00:14:32.320
while maintaining velocity?

00:14:32.320 --> 00:14:34.920
And then how do you handle the risk?

00:14:34.920 --> 00:14:37.780
Like, you want the human involved.

00:14:37.980 --> 00:14:40.780
I presume because it's risk reduction.

00:14:40.780 --> 00:14:41.620
Exactly.

00:14:41.620 --> 00:14:43.940
The human can plug in, okay, I get what's going on here.

00:14:43.940 --> 00:14:45.140
I can see things a little bit differently.

00:14:45.140 --> 00:14:47.900
I can blah, blah, blah that the AI maybe can't do.

00:14:49.340 --> 00:14:52.140
How do you balance human intervention

00:14:52.140 --> 00:14:56.340
with velocity with risk?

00:14:56.340 --> 00:15:00.340
Yeah, so I think those are the three parameters.

00:15:00.340 --> 00:15:01.620
I think it's a really hard question.

00:15:01.620 --> 00:15:03.380
So I can't wait for your answer.

00:15:04.740 --> 00:15:07.140
So let's see, I mean,

00:15:07.180 --> 00:15:09.220
some of the, I'll think through, right, with you.

00:15:09.220 --> 00:15:13.660
Hopefully you can help me with question, you know,

00:15:13.660 --> 00:15:14.500
yeah, anyway.

00:15:14.500 --> 00:15:16.860
I'll interact, but I want to start.

00:15:16.860 --> 00:15:19.380
This is a good place to dwell for a bit.

00:15:19.380 --> 00:15:20.500
Yeah, for sure.

00:15:20.500 --> 00:15:23.240
So the thing is,

00:15:23.240 --> 00:15:27.490
I think the framing that human is a bottleneck

00:15:27.490 --> 00:15:28.970
will always be true

00:15:28.970 --> 00:15:31.930
because that's a constraining resource,

00:15:31.930 --> 00:15:33.770
irrespective of whether you go one X,

00:15:33.770 --> 00:15:36.530
10 X or 100 X, right?

00:15:36.530 --> 00:15:39.130
And today machines have always,

00:15:39.130 --> 00:15:41.120
will always go faster than humans.

00:15:41.120 --> 00:15:42.320
Now the question is,

00:15:44.100 --> 00:15:47.990
how little of a bottleneck can the human be?

00:15:47.990 --> 00:15:50.870
Can the human increase their throughput by 10 X?

00:15:50.870 --> 00:15:52.350
So you're still a bottleneck

00:15:52.350 --> 00:15:54.110
because machines will still move faster,

00:15:54.110 --> 00:15:56.510
but overall throughput is increasing 10 X.

00:15:56.510 --> 00:15:58.390
And I think that's where,

00:15:58.390 --> 00:16:00.150
that's how I would see it.

00:16:00.150 --> 00:16:02.630
We want quality engineers with the people

00:16:02.630 --> 00:16:04.910
and we think of it as release managers,

00:16:04.910 --> 00:16:06.950
people who are taking the risk

00:16:07.390 --> 00:16:10.110
that something can be released into production,

00:16:10.110 --> 00:16:12.550
can do their things 10 X faster

00:16:12.550 --> 00:16:16.030
while keeping risk same or mitigating risk.

00:16:16.030 --> 00:16:18.950
And what are the tools they need to be able to do that

00:16:18.950 --> 00:16:21.190
is how I would think about it, I think.

00:16:21.190 --> 00:16:23.790
And those are the tools I think we are building.

00:16:23.790 --> 00:16:25.190
So if you look at,

00:16:25.190 --> 00:16:27.830
we talk a lot about release readiness

00:16:28.830 --> 00:16:31.070
and all the tools you require for release readiness.

00:16:31.070 --> 00:16:33.350
So for example, I'll give you an example.

00:16:33.350 --> 00:16:34.350
In today's CI, CD,

00:16:34.350 --> 00:16:37.830
so we are not a replacement for security testing.

00:16:37.830 --> 00:16:38.910
You need to get security testing.

00:16:38.910 --> 00:16:40.950
You need to get experts in security

00:16:40.950 --> 00:16:42.070
to security test your app,

00:16:42.070 --> 00:16:44.630
but they do it periodically.

00:16:44.630 --> 00:16:46.990
You can't get them to do it all the time.

00:16:46.990 --> 00:16:48.310
But what you also need

00:16:48.310 --> 00:16:50.710
as somebody who's releasing stuff into production

00:16:50.710 --> 00:16:52.990
is to make sure the wrong SDK,

00:16:52.990 --> 00:16:56.550
wrong version of the SDK with the wrong adapter,

00:16:56.550 --> 00:16:58.190
et cetera, has not gone into production.

00:16:58.190 --> 00:17:00.350
So you need to be able to track those.

00:17:00.350 --> 00:17:04.190
And what trackers have gone into your mobile app

00:17:04.190 --> 00:17:05.630
as part of the SDK.

00:17:05.630 --> 00:17:08.830
So these are things which wouldn't have been a problem

00:17:08.830 --> 00:17:10.310
even a few years ago,

00:17:10.310 --> 00:17:12.430
because they got taken care of

00:17:12.430 --> 00:17:14.470
in your agile development process.

00:17:14.470 --> 00:17:15.870
In agile development process,

00:17:15.870 --> 00:17:18.030
there's handover from A to B to C.

00:17:18.030 --> 00:17:20.230
And in that, you saw exactly what the change was

00:17:20.230 --> 00:17:23.270
in the risk which was being moved from A to B.

00:17:23.270 --> 00:17:25.190
But today, that is not there

00:17:25.190 --> 00:17:27.030
because you're moving much faster, like you said.

00:17:27.030 --> 00:17:28.990
So the key elements,

00:17:28.990 --> 00:17:31.310
making sure let's say a particular tracker

00:17:31.310 --> 00:17:33.470
doesn't go into production by mistake,

00:17:33.470 --> 00:17:35.350
because you used it in your,

00:17:36.820 --> 00:17:37.660
what do you call it,

00:17:37.660 --> 00:17:43.050
in your staging environment for some specific activity.

00:17:43.410 --> 00:17:45.570
Those are things I think become even more important.

00:17:45.570 --> 00:17:46.410
And those are the things

00:17:46.410 --> 00:17:49.730
which the tools need to equip the human

00:17:49.730 --> 00:17:51.210
to make sure the human is comfortable

00:17:51.210 --> 00:17:54.950
going at the 10x speed.

00:17:54.950 --> 00:17:55.790
Yeah.

00:17:55.790 --> 00:17:57.550
So, and that's exactly,

00:17:57.550 --> 00:17:59.230
I think what we need are,

00:17:59.230 --> 00:18:00.470
I'm gonna build on that.

00:18:00.470 --> 00:18:02.950
I think you're on to something.

00:18:02.950 --> 00:18:04.030
I don't know if I,

00:18:04.030 --> 00:18:07.260
I mean, I wish,

00:18:07.260 --> 00:18:08.860
it is a thing where I think you're right,

00:18:08.860 --> 00:18:09.900
but I don't want to admit it.

00:18:09.900 --> 00:18:12.620
And that the human's always going to be the bottleneck.

00:18:12.620 --> 00:18:14.140
I don't want,

00:18:14.140 --> 00:18:17.460
I want bottlenecks or the least bottleneck as possible.

00:18:17.460 --> 00:18:19.900
But where I think in addition to,

00:18:19.900 --> 00:18:22.150
a lot of people are focusing on,

00:18:22.150 --> 00:18:23.790
I'm gonna tangent a little bit here.

00:18:23.790 --> 00:18:25.230
There's a lot of companies like yours

00:18:25.230 --> 00:18:26.670
and other companies looking at testing tools,

00:18:26.670 --> 00:18:27.590
development tools,

00:18:27.590 --> 00:18:29.870
how to build stuff faster

00:18:29.870 --> 00:18:32.070
and then how to test things faster.

00:18:32.070 --> 00:18:34.310
What I spent a chunk of my early,

00:18:34.310 --> 00:18:36.070
like a long time ago writing,

00:18:36.070 --> 00:18:39.590
like we're diagnostic and debugging tools.

00:18:39.590 --> 00:18:42.310
Like help me figure out what's going on.

00:18:42.310 --> 00:18:43.350
Wrote tools,

00:18:43.350 --> 00:18:47.630
oh my God, 20 years ago to deep dive

00:18:47.630 --> 00:18:49.470
into true type fonts when they were brand new

00:18:49.470 --> 00:18:51.750
and be able to like look at all the gray areas

00:18:51.750 --> 00:18:53.790
and figure out why it may look fuzzy

00:18:53.790 --> 00:18:55.150
or whose font may be buggy

00:18:55.150 --> 00:18:57.230
or why this weird font from some weird place

00:18:57.230 --> 00:18:59.030
is crashing, crashing windows.

00:19:01.020 --> 00:19:02.380
What I haven't seen yet,

00:19:02.380 --> 00:19:04.300
and maybe it's because these are happening

00:19:05.140 --> 00:19:06.100
internally and companies are not shipping,

00:19:06.100 --> 00:19:09.980
but the investment cost in ROI

00:19:09.980 --> 00:19:11.620
for building these diagnostic tools

00:19:11.620 --> 00:19:13.260
where I would spend two weeks writing something

00:19:13.260 --> 00:19:15.420
can be done in a couple of hours at most.

00:19:16.560 --> 00:19:21.560
I wonder if the way we reduce the bottleneck of humans

00:19:22.280 --> 00:19:24.520
is to give humans these diagnostic tools

00:19:24.520 --> 00:19:26.200
to help them figure out

00:19:26.200 --> 00:19:28.000
if things are working in the way they expect

00:19:28.000 --> 00:19:29.200
or not faster.

00:19:30.620 --> 00:19:32.260
And I think the answer is yes,

00:19:32.260 --> 00:19:34.570
I just haven't proven it myself yet.

00:19:34.770 --> 00:19:38.370
I'm curious, how much do you think diagnostic

00:19:38.370 --> 00:19:40.290
and debugging tools are going to,

00:19:40.290 --> 00:19:43.560
are those going to become more important?

00:19:43.560 --> 00:19:45.280
Kind of stays something that some teams use

00:19:45.280 --> 00:19:46.440
or don't use?

00:19:46.440 --> 00:19:47.600
What's your opinion on that?

00:19:47.600 --> 00:19:49.240
What happens in that area?

00:19:49.240 --> 00:19:50.360
I have no doubt about it.

00:19:50.360 --> 00:19:53.560
I think it becomes more and more important

00:19:53.560 --> 00:19:55.080
because it just gives the human

00:19:55.080 --> 00:19:58.400
who's moving things forward that much confidence

00:19:58.400 --> 00:20:00.760
that if something goes wrong, there's a fallback.

00:20:00.760 --> 00:20:02.200
And that's where the whole shift left,

00:20:02.200 --> 00:20:04.660
shift right conversation also probably

00:20:05.500 --> 00:20:06.940
comes in where you want to be able to take

00:20:06.940 --> 00:20:08.580
what's happening in production

00:20:08.580 --> 00:20:11.420
and inform what you want to do next

00:20:11.420 --> 00:20:13.300
in development, right?

00:20:13.300 --> 00:20:14.580
You want to see a certain pattern

00:20:14.580 --> 00:20:16.140
which is coming up in production

00:20:16.140 --> 00:20:17.900
and make sure you're testing for that the next time

00:20:17.900 --> 00:20:19.220
if you missed it this time.

00:20:19.220 --> 00:20:22.380
And I think we think a lot about that.

00:20:22.380 --> 00:20:26.460
We think a lot about how you can take data

00:20:26.460 --> 00:20:28.780
from your observability traces

00:20:28.780 --> 00:20:31.900
and map them into testing flows.

00:20:32.860 --> 00:20:35.780
Yes, thank you for bringing that up.

00:20:35.780 --> 00:20:39.900
Yeah, and to make sure that you run that

00:20:39.900 --> 00:20:44.220
from on multiple devices in multiple conditions

00:20:44.220 --> 00:20:45.700
because in mobile you're also running it

00:20:45.700 --> 00:20:46.980
in different conditions, right?

00:20:46.980 --> 00:20:49.500
Whether your handset has enough memory,

00:20:49.500 --> 00:20:51.980
enough bandwidth and so on

00:20:51.980 --> 00:20:54.820
and making sure things are working okay, right?

00:20:54.820 --> 00:20:57.180
And I think we have,

00:20:57.180 --> 00:20:58.300
so I don't think,

00:20:58.300 --> 00:21:00.140
so I think the way to think about it

00:21:00.140 --> 00:21:02.420
from a tool perspective for testing

00:21:03.870 --> 00:21:05.510
because that's my point of view

00:21:05.510 --> 00:21:07.350
and maybe it's too narrow

00:21:07.350 --> 00:21:09.150
but that's still my point of view is

00:21:10.310 --> 00:21:14.900
to be able to integrate with the observability providers

00:21:14.900 --> 00:21:17.660
and match it to specific test runs.

00:21:17.660 --> 00:21:21.580
So we think a lot about looking at memory, CPU,

00:21:21.580 --> 00:21:23.660
why the test run is going on and so on.

00:21:25.340 --> 00:21:26.980
Yeah, and it goes back,

00:21:26.980 --> 00:21:31.050
this is going back many years,

00:21:31.690 --> 00:21:33.530
at least 10 years, 12 years

00:21:33.530 --> 00:21:37.090
when I worked at Microsoft,

00:21:37.090 --> 00:21:39.090
we would have a lot even in our,

00:21:39.090 --> 00:21:40.970
I'm just thinking about Xbox One for example right now

00:21:40.970 --> 00:21:43.970
because I ran across some old email

00:21:43.970 --> 00:21:45.650
about this this weekend

00:21:45.650 --> 00:21:48.930
and we couldn't ship every day.

00:21:48.930 --> 00:21:51.130
We would update our beta users.

00:21:51.130 --> 00:21:52.210
It was a big download

00:21:52.210 --> 00:21:53.610
and download speeds were slower back then.

00:21:53.610 --> 00:21:56.410
So as frequently as they would commit to downloading

00:21:57.370 --> 00:22:02.850
but we collected a lot of information.

00:22:02.850 --> 00:22:04.170
And of course, when we collect information,

00:22:04.170 --> 00:22:05.650
it's not a big upload,

00:22:05.650 --> 00:22:07.130
it's little bits of data

00:22:07.130 --> 00:22:08.850
that help us figure out what's going on.

00:22:08.850 --> 00:22:11.850
But we could tell what features people were using

00:22:11.850 --> 00:22:13.490
and you can do the same thing with mobile today,

00:22:13.490 --> 00:22:14.330
much easier.

00:22:14.330 --> 00:22:16.210
AI can even help you figure out how to do this.

00:22:16.210 --> 00:22:18.250
But the point is if I deliver a new feature

00:22:18.250 --> 00:22:19.730
or a change to a feature

00:22:20.890 --> 00:22:22.410
and this goes onto the development side,

00:22:22.410 --> 00:22:24.810
I can't just think of here's my feature.

00:22:24.970 --> 00:22:27.130
I used to call it forum driven development.

00:22:27.130 --> 00:22:28.290
I'll deliver a new feature

00:22:28.290 --> 00:22:29.610
and wait for people to talk about in the forum

00:22:29.610 --> 00:22:30.610
to see if it's any good or not,

00:22:30.610 --> 00:22:31.850
which is really stupid.

00:22:33.730 --> 00:22:36.010
You wanna have, you wanna be able to,

00:22:36.010 --> 00:22:37.610
I ship a new feature.

00:22:37.610 --> 00:22:41.570
My canonical fake name is shimmery blue menus.

00:22:41.570 --> 00:22:43.570
And I wanna know if people are using them,

00:22:43.570 --> 00:22:44.610
what experience they're having,

00:22:44.610 --> 00:22:46.250
does CPU spike or RAM spike

00:22:46.250 --> 00:22:47.450
when they're using these things.

00:22:47.450 --> 00:22:51.250
And my feature includes all the information,

00:22:51.250 --> 00:22:54.210
all the code to make sure I can collect that data

00:22:54.210 --> 00:22:55.050
and see what's going on.

00:22:55.050 --> 00:22:56.490
Even if I can't,

00:22:56.490 --> 00:22:57.970
like as soon as I deploy,

00:22:57.970 --> 00:22:59.810
I can begin getting that information

00:22:59.810 --> 00:23:01.730
and figuring out what I may need to change

00:23:01.730 --> 00:23:04.730
or adjust or tweak or delete

00:23:04.730 --> 00:23:06.370
before the release the next week

00:23:06.370 --> 00:23:08.530
or whenever the next release goes out.

00:23:08.530 --> 00:23:11.290
I think it's more important than ever,

00:23:11.290 --> 00:23:13.450
just considering the amount of volume that can,

00:23:13.450 --> 00:23:15.330
volume of changes that can go into any update

00:23:15.330 --> 00:23:19.510
of an application that we have the ability

00:23:19.510 --> 00:23:21.870
to understand how customers are using that feature.

00:23:21.870 --> 00:23:23.790
And then as you said,

00:23:23.790 --> 00:23:28.150
pull that directly into how teams approach testing for that.

00:23:28.150 --> 00:23:31.110
Like, oh, here's all the time you hear teams say,

00:23:31.110 --> 00:23:33.890
oh, customers are actually using this

00:23:33.890 --> 00:23:35.490
different than we thought.

00:23:35.490 --> 00:23:38.810
And they sound almost surprised,

00:23:38.810 --> 00:23:39.850
but you shouldn't be surprised

00:23:39.850 --> 00:23:41.210
because we can't pretend they're gonna use it.

00:23:41.210 --> 00:23:42.850
But if you don't have the data,

00:23:42.850 --> 00:23:44.450
you never know and you'd be on optimizing

00:23:44.450 --> 00:23:45.970
for the wrong flow.

00:23:45.970 --> 00:23:48.420
So it becomes super important.

00:23:48.420 --> 00:23:50.300
And if we can drive that back into our testing

00:23:50.300 --> 00:23:52.220
and the automation we create for these apps,

00:23:53.180 --> 00:23:54.360
it's even better because then

00:23:54.360 --> 00:23:56.680
as much as we're not the customer,

00:23:56.680 --> 00:24:01.120
we can at least try and make sure their scenarios

00:24:01.120 --> 00:24:03.370
are working in a way that we're happy with.

00:24:03.370 --> 00:24:04.530
No, I couldn't agree more.

00:24:04.530 --> 00:24:07.490
I mean, that's exactly I think where the tools are going

00:24:07.490 --> 00:24:10.920
because I think as much as they say

00:24:10.920 --> 00:24:14.520
there is a gate for mobile with the app store,

00:24:14.520 --> 00:24:17.520
that doesn't mean that the SDLC process is not changing.

00:24:17.520 --> 00:24:19.400
And I think all of these are the changes

00:24:19.400 --> 00:24:22.080
which are happening as we speak.

00:24:22.720 --> 00:24:23.560
And it's very exciting, I think.

00:24:23.560 --> 00:24:25.800
It's very exciting to be part of this.

00:24:25.800 --> 00:24:27.560
And I think in two years, it'll be very different

00:24:27.560 --> 00:24:30.520
and we'll see how all of this plays out.

00:24:30.520 --> 00:24:31.360
Yeah.

00:24:31.360 --> 00:24:33.760
But yeah, it'll be different, yeah.

00:24:33.760 --> 00:24:35.960
I wanna talk about one challenge I've heard about

