all the automation just works

00:24:49.640 --> 00:24:51.680
and the rest is pretty close.

00:24:52.640 --> 00:24:54.560
That's about as much as I'll say about it.

00:24:54.560 --> 00:24:57.200
And it's getting better now with AI tools, et cetera.

00:24:58.750 --> 00:25:00.310
Famous quote that I can't remember who said

00:25:00.310 --> 00:25:03.390
or what the quote was about abstraction and programming

00:25:03.390 --> 00:25:04.510
and everything's an abstraction

00:25:04.510 --> 00:25:06.430
and people just try and make everything easier.

00:25:06.430 --> 00:25:07.670
And by making things easier,

00:25:07.670 --> 00:25:09.110
they also making more difficult.

00:25:09.110 --> 00:25:13.150
And the latest case in point for that comment

00:25:13.150 --> 00:25:14.660
is Flutter.

00:25:14.660 --> 00:25:16.580
And for those that don't know Flutter

00:25:16.580 --> 00:25:21.180
is Google's UI toolkit for cross-platform apps

00:25:21.180 --> 00:25:23.380
which is great developers that can write once

00:25:23.380 --> 00:25:25.340
one code base renders everywhere.

00:25:25.340 --> 00:25:27.020
Yay, easier.

00:25:27.970 --> 00:25:31.290
Except it's sort of a nightmare for traditional test tools

00:25:31.290 --> 00:25:36.290
because they don't have the same accessibility trees.

00:25:36.730 --> 00:25:40.090
In fact, it's basically painting on the screen

00:25:40.090 --> 00:25:41.530
as far as I can tell.

00:25:41.530 --> 00:25:46.530
And it's a yay for developers

00:25:46.930 --> 00:25:49.290
unless those developers are good developers

00:25:49.290 --> 00:25:50.930
and decide the need to test their code too.

00:25:50.930 --> 00:25:52.250
And we can do model via controller

00:25:52.250 --> 00:25:53.610
and test below the UI level

00:25:53.610 --> 00:25:55.250
which they should be doing anyway.

00:25:55.250 --> 00:25:57.890
But we always want at least a little bit

00:25:57.890 --> 00:25:59.770
testing automation pyramid

00:25:59.770 --> 00:26:01.210
which is just by mentioning that

00:26:01.210 --> 00:26:02.770
people will probably turn off the podcast.

00:26:02.770 --> 00:26:03.770
I'm very sorry.

00:26:03.770 --> 00:26:06.530
It is not, I've always said the test automation pyramid

00:26:06.530 --> 00:26:10.450
is not a desired goal of your automation.

00:26:10.450 --> 00:26:12.250
It's how your automation ends up

00:26:12.250 --> 00:26:14.450
if you have a well-designed application.

00:26:14.450 --> 00:26:16.530
And I'm sorry.

00:26:16.530 --> 00:26:17.730
I hope I didn't hurt anybody's feelings

00:26:17.730 --> 00:26:18.570
for bringing it up.

00:26:19.450 --> 00:26:21.410
You always want to write a little bit of UI automation,

00:26:21.410 --> 00:26:22.410
those end-to-end tests.

00:26:22.410 --> 00:26:24.050
I wouldn't base all of your testing on that

00:26:24.050 --> 00:26:27.410
because then you're in so much trouble.

00:26:27.410 --> 00:26:29.210
But anyway, back to the topic.

00:26:30.250 --> 00:26:32.610
Flutter is awesome as an app developer

00:26:32.610 --> 00:26:33.730
and a pain in the butt

00:26:33.730 --> 00:26:35.530
when they try and test their code.

00:26:37.010 --> 00:26:37.850
Talk a little bit about that.

00:26:37.850 --> 00:26:39.330
I know QA palette helps with that,

00:26:39.330 --> 00:26:40.570
but talk about the challenge

00:26:40.570 --> 00:26:44.770
and how teams just kind of tackle that in general.

00:26:44.770 --> 00:26:48.090
Yeah, no, Flutter for testers is a nightmare, right?

00:26:48.090 --> 00:26:50.890
Because it breaks everything you've done

00:26:50.890 --> 00:26:54.250
to test React Native apps on native apps, right?

00:26:54.250 --> 00:26:55.770
Like you said, it's a painting.

00:26:55.770 --> 00:27:01.310
And therefore, it is rendering widgets

00:27:01.910 --> 00:27:03.950
rather than elements and so on, right?

00:27:03.950 --> 00:27:07.070
As you do in a traditional app.

00:27:07.070 --> 00:27:09.830
And within the widgets, you have locator elements

00:27:09.830 --> 00:27:11.150
which need locators.

00:27:11.150 --> 00:27:14.190
And none of these are actually available on Flutter.

00:27:14.190 --> 00:27:17.510
So either the developer gives IDs of some sort

00:27:17.550 --> 00:27:19.150
which you can handle.

00:27:19.150 --> 00:27:23.620
And then, or you need to be able to create these IDs

00:27:23.620 --> 00:27:25.940
as a testing tool on the fly

00:27:25.940 --> 00:27:28.580
with a reasonable degree of confidence

00:27:28.580 --> 00:27:30.180
that they won't change when you open it

00:27:30.180 --> 00:27:31.660
in the next device.

00:27:31.660 --> 00:27:34.300
And I think that's something we've learned the hard way.

00:27:34.300 --> 00:27:37.740
Like I said, my co-founder runs a company

00:27:37.740 --> 00:27:40.500
which builds mobile apps for large enterprises

00:27:40.500 --> 00:27:41.860
and some of them are Flutter, right?

00:27:41.860 --> 00:27:45.260
So you had first-hand experience in testing Flutter apps

00:27:45.260 --> 00:27:47.340
and through a lot of trial and error,

00:27:47.340 --> 00:27:49.820
I think you've understood what kind of locators

00:27:49.820 --> 00:27:55.170
to assign to different modes of Flutter apps, essentially.

00:27:56.210 --> 00:27:59.130
And so some of it is visual, some of it is hybrid,

00:27:59.130 --> 00:28:03.530
some of it is the ID which is given by the developer.

00:28:03.530 --> 00:28:06.290
And a combination of that essentially works

00:28:06.290 --> 00:28:07.530
reasonably well.

00:28:07.530 --> 00:28:08.690
It breaks once in a while,

00:28:08.690 --> 00:28:11.130
but I think it works quite well.

00:28:11.130 --> 00:28:13.690
Now, there are other problems with Flutter as well, right?

00:28:13.690 --> 00:28:14.970
The other problem with Flutter is

00:28:14.970 --> 00:28:16.330
you still have to interact with the app

00:28:16.330 --> 00:28:17.810
and you go through App PM.

00:28:17.810 --> 00:28:21.410
And so a simple thing like set value

00:28:21.410 --> 00:28:24.210
is something which you do as part of a testing flow.

00:28:24.210 --> 00:28:26.690
It works very differently in Flutter

00:28:26.690 --> 00:28:29.330
versus a React Native or a Native application.

00:28:29.330 --> 00:28:30.370
And therefore, when you're working

00:28:30.370 --> 00:28:31.490
with the Flutter application,

00:28:31.490 --> 00:28:33.930
you need essentially a different middleware

00:28:33.930 --> 00:28:35.650
between your application and App PM

00:28:35.650 --> 00:28:37.210
and then the mobile app

00:28:37.210 --> 00:28:40.330
for the whole automation flow to work well.

00:28:40.330 --> 00:28:42.730
And I think that's essentially

00:28:42.730 --> 00:28:44.290
what we've spent a lot of time building,

00:28:44.290 --> 00:28:45.890
the middleware.

00:28:46.050 --> 00:28:48.170
It works well, we continue to improve it.

00:28:48.170 --> 00:28:52.700
And I think our customers are very happy with this

00:28:52.700 --> 00:28:54.300
because there's nothing else in the market

00:28:54.300 --> 00:28:58.700
which allows you to do black box Flutter testing.

00:28:58.700 --> 00:29:02.700
And I say black box because a lot of the testing tools

00:29:02.700 --> 00:29:06.300
and the ecosystem around Flutter are all gray box

00:29:06.300 --> 00:29:08.540
because they're essentially developer led.

00:29:08.540 --> 00:29:10.460
Developers love gray box tools.

00:29:10.460 --> 00:29:12.900
And then there isn't a black box tool out there.

00:29:12.900 --> 00:29:15.820
And ours is, I would say still a work in progress,

00:29:15.860 --> 00:29:18.690
but very good, yeah.

00:29:18.690 --> 00:29:20.650
Yeah, well, and you know I believe

00:29:20.650 --> 00:29:21.850
that developers should write

00:29:21.850 --> 00:29:24.050
the vast majority of automation.

00:29:24.050 --> 00:29:25.170
So I'm totally okay with that,

00:29:25.170 --> 00:29:29.250
but there are some scenarios

00:29:29.250 --> 00:29:30.410
that are good to run once in a while.

00:29:30.410 --> 00:29:35.370
So I think that,

00:29:35.370 --> 00:29:37.370
yeah, I think having both is good.

00:29:38.610 --> 00:29:41.090
It's still, yeah, there's,

00:29:43.760 --> 00:29:45.320
yeah, I'll leave it at that

00:29:45.320 --> 00:29:46.160
without getting too opinion

00:29:46.200 --> 00:29:49.000
because I wanna, we have plenty of time left,

00:29:49.000 --> 00:29:51.240
but I have a topic that's gonna take a while.

00:29:51.240 --> 00:29:54.680
We cannot do an AI testing podcast.

00:29:54.680 --> 00:29:56.120
I mean, I'm gonna replace Brent

00:29:56.120 --> 00:29:58.160
with someone with an I first name.

00:29:58.160 --> 00:30:00.320
Ian, if you're out there, you're hired.

00:30:02.030 --> 00:30:07.360
How does, so many topics on AI we can talk about.

00:30:07.360 --> 00:30:08.840
Agentic AI is something I've been playing

00:30:08.840 --> 00:30:12.040
with quite a bit or a little bit at least.

00:30:12.040 --> 00:30:14.280
Super interesting, super fun.

00:30:15.280 --> 00:30:19.850
What is, I think your product does this,

00:30:19.850 --> 00:30:23.250
but what does, what is the impact of,

00:30:23.250 --> 00:30:25.210
or what is agentic AI in testing?

00:30:25.210 --> 00:30:29.210
Like how should we use agentic AI in general to help?

00:30:29.210 --> 00:30:31.060
I'm not even saying testing

00:30:31.060 --> 00:30:32.740
because I care much more about quality

00:30:32.740 --> 00:30:33.980
than I do about testing.

00:30:33.980 --> 00:30:36.180
So how do you use agentic AI to reduce risk

00:30:36.180 --> 00:30:37.380
and make sure that we're building

00:30:37.380 --> 00:30:40.080
a quality product for our customers?

00:30:40.080 --> 00:30:43.740
Yeah, so maybe we take a step back, right,

00:30:43.740 --> 00:30:47.060
about AI in general, in AI and then we'll come back.

00:30:47.060 --> 00:30:48.340
Step backs are good.

00:30:48.340 --> 00:30:53.560
Yeah, and so the way I think about AI, right?

00:30:54.960 --> 00:30:59.360
AI is essentially great at pattern matching.

00:30:59.360 --> 00:31:01.760
It's one of the things it does really well.

00:31:01.760 --> 00:31:04.680
I call it very, very fancy autocomplete.

00:31:04.680 --> 00:31:08.780
Yeah, and a lot of the innovation

00:31:08.780 --> 00:31:12.180
has been in using this ability in different ways

00:31:12.180 --> 00:31:14.820
to get, to predict the next token and so on, right?

00:31:15.460 --> 00:31:18.660
And then you create content, you create all kinds of things.

00:31:18.660 --> 00:31:24.720
Now, I think actually the recency bias,

00:31:24.840 --> 00:31:27.600
you know, the CEO of Palo Alto Networks, right,

00:31:27.600 --> 00:31:32.600
Nikesh Arora, he had a great message on X

00:31:33.560 --> 00:31:37.810
or Twitter yesterday where he spoke about

00:31:37.810 --> 00:31:43.260
the current AI tug of war, so to say,

00:31:43.340 --> 00:31:44.940
is between breadth and depth

00:31:44.940 --> 00:31:46.380
because at a consumer level,

00:31:46.380 --> 00:31:49.860
people think AI is great because consumers need breadth.

00:31:49.860 --> 00:31:51.300
You want to be able to ask whatever you want

00:31:51.300 --> 00:31:53.570
to get a reasonably good answer, you're fine.

00:31:53.570 --> 00:31:56.450
But the enterprise use cases, you need depth

00:31:56.450 --> 00:31:59.850
and you need a very high degree of confidence.

00:31:59.850 --> 00:32:01.970
And that's where the risk parameters change

00:32:01.970 --> 00:32:04.090
and that's where you bring in FDEs,

00:32:04.090 --> 00:32:05.530
you bring in all these guardrails,

00:32:05.530 --> 00:32:07.130
you're building all this scaffolding.

00:32:07.130 --> 00:32:08.250
It takes a lot of time to get

00:32:08.250 --> 00:32:10.090
an enterprise use case correctly.

00:32:10.090 --> 00:32:12.690
And I think that feeds into testing, right?

00:32:12.690 --> 00:32:15.330
Because testing is essentially about risk

00:32:15.330 --> 00:32:16.730
and you don't want to add a tool

00:32:16.730 --> 00:32:20.170
which adds 10 or 20% of risk or variability

00:32:20.170 --> 00:32:22.330
to the risk you want to reduce.

00:32:22.330 --> 00:32:24.650
So I think at a high level, that's what it is.

00:32:24.650 --> 00:32:27.450
Now, what can AI do well nevertheless, right?

00:32:27.450 --> 00:32:28.890
Because it's a new paradigm.

00:32:28.890 --> 00:32:31.710
And the way I think about it is that

00:32:31.710 --> 00:32:36.940
over a period of time, testing and risk verification,

00:32:38.380 --> 00:32:40.460
this whole industry has come up

00:32:40.460 --> 00:32:43.340
with its own nomenclature to talk to each other

00:32:43.340 --> 00:32:48.660
to interact, to manage auditability,

00:32:48.980 --> 00:32:53.180
to manage risk, to manage consistency and reliability.

00:32:53.180 --> 00:32:55.420
And one of them is the BDD format, right?

00:32:55.420 --> 00:32:57.700
Yeah, it's easy to understand things, right?

00:32:57.700 --> 00:33:01.140
So the way we use AI, for example,

00:33:01.140 --> 00:33:04.980
is to, we've got all these adapters

00:33:04.980 --> 00:33:07.860
which convert your FSD, your PRD,

00:33:07.860 --> 00:33:11.220
your test cases written in your test case management tool

00:33:11.220 --> 00:33:12.460
into BDD.

00:33:12.460 --> 00:33:15.220
And we don't call it BDD, we call it predictive BDD

00:33:15.220 --> 00:33:18.540
because it's what we predicted from our tool.

00:33:18.540 --> 00:33:20.340
And we present it, and again,

00:33:20.340 --> 00:33:21.900
coming back to what I said about making

00:33:21.900 --> 00:33:24.100
the quality engineer the superhero,

00:33:24.100 --> 00:33:25.500
we have to present it to the quality engineer

00:33:25.500 --> 00:33:28.260
in a way in which they can verify quickly

00:33:28.260 --> 00:33:30.260
so they 10x their volume, right?

00:33:30.260 --> 00:33:32.220
So that's how I would think about,

00:33:32.220 --> 00:33:35.220
that's one way in which you can use AI in testing

00:33:35.220 --> 00:33:37.180
without adding to the risk

00:33:37.180 --> 00:33:40.340
because anytime you use AI, you're adding risk

00:33:40.380 --> 00:33:42.940
because there's a variability, inherent variability.

00:33:42.940 --> 00:33:46.170
So coming back to this primitive, so to say,

00:33:46.170 --> 00:33:48.490
a BDD or knowledge graphs and so on,

00:33:48.490 --> 00:33:50.150
helps with that.

00:33:50.150 --> 00:33:50.990
Yeah.

00:33:50.990 --> 00:33:52.550
And then just, I'm going to,

00:33:52.550 --> 00:33:54.030
I need a fake name for this,

00:33:54.030 --> 00:33:56.830
but a little translation,

00:33:56.830 --> 00:33:59.070
because actually not everyone listed the podcast

00:33:59.070 --> 00:34:02.630
I found out is our tech folks necessarily,

00:34:02.630 --> 00:34:04.670
or tech adjacent a lot of times,

00:34:04.670 --> 00:34:07.190
but BDD is behavior-driven development

00:34:07.190 --> 00:34:10.590
where you're trying to describe the behavior

00:34:11.430 --> 00:34:12.790
first and then you implement the code

00:34:12.790 --> 00:34:15.790
to make that behavior occur.

00:34:15.790 --> 00:34:16.990
So that's what BDD is.

00:34:16.990 --> 00:34:17.830
You mentioned PRD,

00:34:17.830 --> 00:34:19.430
which is a product requirements doc.

00:34:19.430 --> 00:34:21.150
It's just a requirements doc.

00:34:21.150 --> 00:34:26.250
So I like to translate the TLAs for everyone,

00:34:26.610 --> 00:34:27.610
just to double check,

00:34:27.610 --> 00:34:28.850
make sure we're on the same page,

00:34:28.850 --> 00:34:31.610
but yeah, that makes sense.

00:34:31.610 --> 00:34:36.870
So one thing, a couple episodes ago,

00:34:36.870 --> 00:34:37.710
when Brent was back

00:34:37.710 --> 00:34:39.390
and we're going to get him a new microphone

00:34:39.390 --> 00:34:40.430
and continue a conversation,

00:34:40.430 --> 00:34:41.270
we're talking about changing

00:34:41.270 --> 00:34:42.430
the modern testing principles

00:34:42.430 --> 00:34:44.110
towards some AI principles.

00:34:44.110 --> 00:34:46.190
And one thing we're talking about,

00:34:46.190 --> 00:34:47.030
and you mentioned,

00:34:47.030 --> 00:34:47.870
I want to hear you dive

00:34:47.870 --> 00:34:49.390
into a little bit deeper,

00:34:49.390 --> 00:34:50.990
is trustworthiness.

00:34:52.430 --> 00:34:53.830
AI can create all this code.

00:34:53.830 --> 00:34:55.190
AI can test all this code.

00:34:55.190 --> 00:34:56.830
AI can evaluate a bunch of stuff.

00:34:56.830 --> 00:34:58.510
AI can look at the customer workflows.

00:34:58.510 --> 00:35:02.530
AI can give us all kinds of information.

00:35:02.530 --> 00:35:03.530
Keeping in mind,

00:35:03.530 --> 00:35:05.370
AI is just a fancy autocomplete

00:35:05.370 --> 00:35:07.850
or a parrot that puts together words

00:35:07.850 --> 00:35:09.690
and code based on what it seemed before.

00:35:09.690 --> 00:35:11.130
So it's a little frightening.

00:35:12.130 --> 00:35:13.890
So we lose some trustworthiness

00:35:13.890 --> 00:35:15.370
on the development side.

00:35:15.370 --> 00:35:17.730
We have to trust what created is good.

00:35:17.730 --> 00:35:19.250
Then we have to trust the tests

00:35:19.250 --> 00:35:21.610
that AI created to make sure they're actually,

00:35:21.610 --> 00:35:24.250
there's a lot of blind faith going on here.

00:35:24.250 --> 00:35:26.810
How do we get over that trustworthiness gap?

00:35:26.810 --> 00:35:27.730
What do we need to do

00:35:27.730 --> 00:35:29.650
to make sure that what we're delivering now

00:35:29.650 --> 00:35:32.210
at lightning speed for millions of people

00:35:32.210 --> 00:35:33.450
is actually trustworthy

00:35:33.450 --> 00:35:36.890
and isn't going to attack us in our sleep?

00:35:36.890 --> 00:35:38.890
So I don't think I have a single answer,

00:35:38.890 --> 00:35:42.250
but I think there are a lot of things which are-

00:35:42.250 --> 00:35:43.450
If there was a single answer,

00:35:43.450 --> 00:35:45.490
I would just go sell that and be rich.

00:35:46.650 --> 00:35:47.490
But I'm not.

00:35:49.490 --> 00:35:51.410
So, I mean, there are a lot of building blocks, right?

00:35:51.410 --> 00:35:54.730
We did speak about how the fix forward,

00:35:54.730 --> 00:35:57.090
the SRE bit feeds into this, right?

00:35:57.090 --> 00:35:58.410
The ability to be able to see

00:35:58.410 --> 00:36:01.090
what the user is doing and react quickly

00:36:01.090 --> 00:36:02.850
reduces some of the risk

00:36:02.850 --> 00:36:06.610
which we are adding to make this whole thing faster.

00:36:06.610 --> 00:36:10.530
And I truly believe that it's essentially a design problem

00:36:10.530 --> 00:36:12.450
to make sure that you surface

00:36:12.450 --> 00:36:14.290
the right things to the human

00:36:14.290 --> 00:36:16.410
so that the human can take action

00:36:16.410 --> 00:36:18.210
either after it's gone into production

00:36:18.210 --> 00:36:19.490
or before it goes into production

00:36:19.490 --> 00:36:21.410
if you're a mobile app.

00:36:21.410 --> 00:36:22.490
And I think there are some of the ways

00:36:22.490 --> 00:36:26.160
in which the systems are improving.

00:36:26.160 --> 00:36:28.080
There are also all these frameworks, right?

00:36:28.080 --> 00:36:30.120
A lot of the reason why,

00:36:30.120 --> 00:36:31.960
like a lot of JavaScript frameworks,

00:36:31.960 --> 00:36:33.760
you would use them in development.

00:36:33.760 --> 00:36:35.480
The part of the reason you would use these frameworks

00:36:35.480 --> 00:36:37.240
is because there is risk mitigation

00:36:38.280 --> 00:36:40.480
when you're shipping things at scale.

00:36:40.480 --> 00:36:41.960
So I think some of these things also,

00:36:41.960 --> 00:36:45.400
I think we'll see some really cool frameworks coming

00:36:45.400 --> 00:36:49.320
which could essentially mitigate certain types of risks

00:36:49.320 --> 00:36:51.560
which are AI specific, right?

00:36:51.560 --> 00:36:54.240
Which we're not able to solve in other ways.

00:36:54.240 --> 00:36:55.280
Now, I don't know what they are,

00:36:55.280 --> 00:36:56.560
but you see them happening.

00:36:56.560 --> 00:36:59.120
So for example, when WebRTC came in

00:36:59.120 --> 00:37:00.920
and there were a new type of interacting

00:37:00.920 --> 00:37:01.880
